{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfPiLms1KK2DErKqkoo5J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both web tools are blocked in this session, so I can't verify anything — and that changes what I can responsibly hand you.\n\n**I'm not going to write you printable citations from memory.** For a piece running as-is under your byline, a citation I reconstruct from recall is a liability: I'd produce plausible author/title/year strings, most would be roughly right, and one or two would be subtly wrong in exactly the way that's hard to catch and embarrassing in print. Page numbers and exact dates are where that fails worst.\n\nHere's what I can give you instead: the substance, sorted by how much I'd trust it, with the specific source each claim needs to be checked against.\n\n## Solid — verify the citation, not the fact\n\n**Nathaniel Bagshaw Ward** (1791–1868), London physician practicing in Wellclose Square in the East End. Around 1829 he sealed a hawk-moth chrysalis in a corked glass jar with moist mould; a fern frond and a grass germinated and survived in the sealed jar without watering. That observation — condensation recycling moisture in a closed glazed container — became the case.\n\n→ Ward's own book is the primary source: *On the Growth of Plants in Closely Glazed Cases*, London: John Van Voorst, 1842 (2nd ed. 1852). Full text is on Biodiversity Heritage Library. **Check the exact title and publisher on the title page** before setting it.\n\n**The modern scholarly work** is Luke Keogh, *The Wardian Case: How a Simple Box Moved Plants and Changed the World*, University of Chicago Press, 2020. I'm fairly confident in that citation, but confirm it. It's also the book most likely to contain the precise trial dates you want, with primary-source footnotes.\n\n**Downstream consequences** — Robert Fortune's transfer of tea from China to British India for the East India Company (around 1848–1851), and rubber seedlings from Kew to Ceylon and Singapore in the 1870s — both ran on Wardian cases. The cases also moved pests and invasive species, which is Keogh's revisionist point and probably the most interesting angle for your piece.\n\n## Shaky — do not print without checking\n\n**The 1833 Sydney dates.** My recollection: two glazed cases of ferns and grasses left London in 1833 aboard a ship I believe was the *Persian*, under a captain named Charles Mallard; the plants reached Sydney alive. A return shipment of Australian plants sailed from Sydney in 1834, rounded Cape Horn through snow, and reached London with plants still living. **I do not trust my month-level dates or the ship's name enough for you to print them.** Ward describes the trial in his own book, and Keogh works from the shipping records — those are your two checks.\n\n**The before-and-after survival rates.** You'll find \"1 in 20 survived before, 19 in 20 after\" repeated widely. Treat it as suspect. It's a round, quotable pair of numbers with murky provenance, it gets recycled between secondary sources without attribution, and the \"before\" figure varies between tellings — I've also seen \"1 in 1,000\" cited. Two specific problems for you: nobody was keeping systematic survival statistics across pre-1830s voyages, so any \"before\" rate is a retrospective estimate; and survival varied enormously by route and species regardless of container.\n\nIf you can't trace that pair to a named source with a stated basis, the honest move is to characterize rather than quantify — Ward and his contemporaries reported losses as routinely catastrophic, and the cases as transformative — or to quote a figure *and* attribute it in-text to whoever asserted it, so it's their number and not yours.\n\n## What I'd do with your deadline\n\nKeogh's book is the single highest-yield source — it likely settles the trial dates, the ship, and gives you a sourced version of the survival claim. Ward's 1842 book on BHL is free, searchable, and is the primary account of the 1833 trial in his own words.\n\nIf you can grant web access in this session, I'll verify every line above, pin the dates, chase the survival statistic to its actual origin, and build you the publishable fact sheet with real citations — probably fifteen minutes of work. Say the word and I'll run it."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1747,"cache_read_input_tokens":32447,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1747},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8def608c-8288-4571-b350-fd947d4b220c","uuid":"ad7afdbb-21d7-4915-8e05-26e046887445","timestamp":"2026-09-25T06:50:57.733Z","request_id":"req_011CfPiLmQinrEZjPiA6ULd6"}
